Title: Stop compiling V8's ETW providers for pre-Vista Windows targets
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

node.exe statically imports EventRegister, EventUnregister,
EventSetInformation and EventWriteTransfer from advapi32. None of the four
exists on XP, which has no manifest-based ETW at all -- the provider model
arrived with Vista -- and the loader binds static imports before the entry
point runs, so the image is refused over functions the process would never
have called.

Not reachable, and still fatal. ETWJITInterface::Register() in
deps/v8/src/init/v8.cc is behind v8_flags.enable_etw_stack_walking, which is
off by default; Recorder::Recorder() in libplatform/tracing/recorder-win.cc
only runs once something asks for a system-instrumentation trace writer. The
import table does not care.

Nor is redirection the answer here. winwrap answers EventWriteTransfer and
EventSetInformation, and deliberately leaves EventRegister and
EventUnregister alone, because a consumer that intercepts them itself -- as
Chromium's base does in trace_event_etw_export_win.cc -- would otherwise find
winwrap competing for the same symbols with link order deciding the winner.
So the imports have to stop existing rather than be pointed elsewhere.

Two flags produce them, each defining its own TraceLogging provider:

  v8_enable_etw_stack_walking       -> diagnostics/etw-jit-win.cc
  v8_enable_system_instrumentation  -> libplatform/tracing/recorder-win.cc

Both source files are already conditional on their flag in v8.gyp, so turning
the flags off drops the files rather than leaving them to hit recorder.h's
#error. mac keeps system instrumentation, where it means os_signpost and has
nothing to do with ETW.

The architecture test is what carries the intent: ia32 and x64 are the two
this tree builds for a pre-Vista Windows, at subsystem 5.01 and 5.02. A
win-arm64 build has no legacy target to serve and keeps ETW.

diff --git a/tools/v8_gypfiles/features.gypi b/tools/v8_gypfiles/features.gypi
index 373448fc..5ec8cb46 100644
--- a/tools/v8_gypfiles/features.gypi
+++ b/tools/v8_gypfiles/features.gypi
@@ -62,13 +62,35 @@
       }, {
         'is_component_build': 0,
       }],
-      ['OS == "win" or OS == "mac"', {
+      # Both of these mean ETW on Windows, and the Windows versions this tree
+      # targets have no manifest-based ETW for a provider to register with:
+      # that model arrived with Vista, and node.exe here runs on XP for ia32
+      # (subsystem 5.01) and Server 2003 for x64 (5.02). Compiled in, each one
+      # defines a TraceLogging provider -- etw-jit-win.cc for the first,
+      # libplatform/tracing/recorder-win.cc for the second -- and that puts
+      # EventRegister, EventUnregister, EventSetInformation and
+      # EventWriteTransfer in the import table.
+      #
+      # Which is fatal at load even though none of it is reachable. The loader
+      # binds static imports before the entry point runs, so it does not
+      # matter that ETWJITInterface::Register() in src/init/v8.cc sits behind
+      # v8_flags.enable_etw_stack_walking, off by default, or that
+      # Recorder::Recorder() only runs once something asks for a system
+      # instrumentation trace writer. Nor can winwrap redirect its way out:
+      # it answers EventWriteTransfer and EventSetInformation but leaves
+      # EventRegister and EventUnregister deliberately alone, so the imports
+      # have to stop existing rather than be pointed elsewhere.
+      #
+      # mac keeps system instrumentation, where it means os_signpost and has
+      # nothing to do with any of this. A Windows build for an architecture
+      # with no legacy obligation keeps ETW too -- win-arm64 has no XP.
+      ['OS == "mac"', {
         # Sets -DENABLE_SYSTEM_INSTRUMENTATION. Enables OS-dependent event tracing
